<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarization requires.

	Deliberately empty. The two capabilities that matter to this app need no
	entitlement:
	  * Accessibility (the synthetic ⌘V paste) is a TCC grant, not an
	    entitlement — the user approves it in System Settings.
	  * Metal runtime shader compilation works fine under the hardened runtime.

	If notarization passes but the signed build crashes on launch, add the
	narrowest entitlement that fixes it and note why:
	  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation  — loading a dylib
	      signed by someone else (unlikely; we link statically)
	  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit                   — a JIT mapping
	  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events        — driving another app
	      via AppleEvents (also needs an NSAppleEventsUsageDescription)
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
